WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
Movies have become so reliant on digital&nbsp;
effects that their original footage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.920
is often completely unrecognizable&nbsp;
from what you see on screen. Here's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.980
what these movies really looked like&nbsp;
before the special effects were added.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:16.620
Leading man Leonardo DiCaprio and director&nbsp;
Alejandro G. Iñárritu weren't the only ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:21.480
to gain recognition for their work on 2015's&nbsp;
The Revenant. From riding a horse off a cliff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.920
to an epic bear attack, ILM, the digital&nbsp;
team behind the film's special effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.800
had their work cut out for them.&nbsp;
The true hero of this film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.002
though, was Glenn Enniss, the 6'4''&nbsp;
stuntman who played the iconic bear.

00:00:33.002 --> 00:00:35.580
"It was hard on the body. So, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.534
on your hands and feet, picking&nbsp;
someone up, throwing them around."

00:00:38.534 --> 00:00:42.960
Ennis also admitted that DiCaprio was a&nbsp;
pretty intense co-star. The A-lister's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.440
agony was so believable that Ennis&nbsp;
thought he was actually hurting him.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:53.160
Despite failing to recoup its $110 million&nbsp;
budget, nobody could deny that 2016's Passengers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.900
looked great. Director Morten Tyldum wanted to&nbsp;
keep the visual effects as subtle as possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:03.060
Effects company MPC was behind many of the&nbsp;
film's iconic shots, including a beautiful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:07.260
zero-gravity water sequence. However,&nbsp;
though special effects may be accessible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:13.080
they certainly aren't cheap. MPC's visual&nbsp;
effects supervisor Pete Dionne told Art of VFX:

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:17.580
"[Morten's] aesthetic is definitely biased&nbsp;
towards invisible effects, and where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.052
couldn't be invisible by nature, to keep them&nbsp;
restrained and to never distract from the drama."

00:01:22.052 --> 00:01:27.720
Warner Bros. maxed out their $250 million&nbsp;
budget for Batman V Superman: Dawn of Justice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.920
and although the film made tons of money,&nbsp;
critics still weren't impressed. Director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.880
Zack Snyder received a lot of flack, but&nbsp;
he claimed that he and his effects team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.620
actually went out of their way to pay homage&nbsp;
to the source material. Take Superman's cape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:44.640
for instance. VFX supervisor&nbsp;
Bryan Hirota told Art of VFX:

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.380
"[It] had to act and feel natural, but on the&nbsp;
other hand behave in an art-directed way and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.741
create appealing shapes that remind&nbsp;
the viewer of the original comics."

00:01:52.741 --> 00:01:57.300
That may sound like a nice detail, but it's a&nbsp;
safe bet that everyone was too busy trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:02.101
spot actor Henry Cavill's CGI-erased moustache&nbsp;
in Justice League to go back and notice.

00:02:02.101 --> 00:02:05.220
The Hunger Games franchise split&nbsp;
its final book into two movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:10.440
and most fans agree that the second was far&nbsp;
more exciting than the first. VFX supervisor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:15.780
Charles Gibson described Mockingjay - Part 2&nbsp;
as a war film with massive setpieces, saying:

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.200
"We had to enhance battle action&nbsp;
and create believable environments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.060
that would hold up for extended coverage."

00:02:21.301 --> 00:02:24.120
Working closely with production&nbsp;
designer Phil Messina,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:28.140
Gibson agreed on a mix of shooting on&nbsp;
location and building digital sets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:32.534
saying that Messina's input on the project&nbsp;
and his design approach were invaluable.

00:02:32.534 --> 00:02:37.800
South Korean director Bong Joon-ho turned&nbsp;
countless Netflix users vegan in 2017 with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:43.440
Okja, the touching story of a girl and her&nbsp;
super-pig. VFX supervisor Erik-Jan de Boer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.520
explained that the success of the film hinged&nbsp;
on whether the audience believed that Okja,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.331
a genetically engineered pig, was real.&nbsp;
As he explained to No Film School,

00:02:51.331 --> 00:02:55.740
"The only way that people are really going&nbsp;
to believe Mija and Okja and their little pet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:00.000
friendship is if we have a lot of contact,&nbsp;
and if that contact is truly believable."

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.400
In order to achieve this physical contact, the VFX&nbsp;
team created a range of foam props for young star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:10.320
Ahn Seo-hyun to interact with, including a full&nbsp;
body suit for the most intimate interactions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:15.600
Fortunately, the effort paid off, as Okja struck&nbsp;
a chord with both viewers and critics alike.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.280
The sequel to Ridley Scott's seminal sci-fi&nbsp;
classic Blade Runner was a long time coming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:23.580
and the VFX team felt the weight&nbsp;
of the '80s cult classic on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:28.140
their shoulders. Overall visual effects&nbsp;
supervisor John Nelson told Art of VFX,

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.900
"Any fan of science fiction or cinema has a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.960
deal of reverence for the original&nbsp;
Blade Runner, myself included."

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:39.900
Blade Runner 2049's cityscapes, in particular,&nbsp;
were a huge job, and multiple VFX companies were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:45.120
hired to craft this world in miniature. While the&nbsp;
film fell below box office expectations, it did at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:50.102
least manage to capture the neon-noir feeling that&nbsp;
resonated with fans of the original so strongly.

00:03:50.102 --> 00:03:55.260
Before Blade Runner 2049, Denis Villeneuve&nbsp;
directed a sci-fi outing that was a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:59.580
more grounded in reality. 2016's&nbsp;
Arrival follows a linguist and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:03.960
physicist as they attempt to communicate&nbsp;
with an alien species. The VFX style was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.740
the polar opposite of the Blade Runner&nbsp;
sequel, seeming drab and washed-out in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:13.260
comparison. VFX supervisor and regular&nbsp;
collaborator Louis Morin told VFXblog,

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:16.440
"[Villeneuve] wanted nothing to&nbsp;
do with any kind of sci-fi movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.500
that we've been seeing recently from&nbsp;
Hollywood. . . . he just wanted this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:24.151
movie to feel so real and so boring in&nbsp;
so many ways, like, ordinary looking."

00:04:24.151 --> 00:04:27.000
Louis then talked about a&nbsp;
cold, cloudy day in Montreal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.180
where Villeneuve explained that this&nbsp;
was what he wanted Arrival to look like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.080
Considering how grey and drab the film&nbsp;
looked, it's safe to say he nailed it.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:39.300
Alex Garland's indie sci-fi drama Ex Machina&nbsp;
beat out many big-budget films to win the Best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:45.540
Achievement in Visual Effects Oscar in 2016.&nbsp;
According to VFX supervisor Andrew Whitehurst,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:50.640
the shoot was so well-organized and efficient&nbsp;
that they had extra cash to spend on making Ava,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.660
the AI, look amazing. He explained that the&nbsp;
initial budget would have only allowed them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:58.860
to animate Ava's torso and limbs, and that the&nbsp;
extra money let them animate her head and neck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:04.080
as well. Garland also wanted to make sure Ava had&nbsp;
romantic appeal. Costume designer Sammy Sheldon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.880
Differ explained that her suit, which left little&nbsp;
to the imagination, was a massive group effort.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:14.760
Zack Snyder's 300 made heavy use of CGI, and&nbsp;
the technology used to create it had advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:18.600
immensely by the time its sequel, Rise of&nbsp;
an Empire, came around. Which, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:23.640
meant squeezing every ounce of that tech into&nbsp;
the movie. Director Noam Murro told Forbes:

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:28.860
"It's amazing how the tools available eight years&nbsp;
later continue to develop. A major difference is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:34.260
CGI and the ability to create things in post that&nbsp;
are convincing and complex and three-dimensional.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:38.880
The idea of creating a water movie without&nbsp;
a drop of water on the set is remarkable."

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:41.340
The filmmaker also revealed they relied on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:45.240
some of the same techniques used in the&nbsp;
Oscar-winning CGI blockbuster Gravity.
 &nbsp;

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:50.640
Neill Blomkamp's follow-up to his acclaimed&nbsp;
debut District 9 didn't exactly go as planned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:55.200
despite the star power of Matt Damon.&nbsp;
The blockbuster sci-fi cost a whopping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:00.540
$115 million to make, but only returned&nbsp;
$93 million at the domestic box office.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:04.560
But while Blomkamp took full&nbsp;
responsibility for the film's failings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.340
the South African director didn't have any&nbsp;
complaints about the special effects team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:13.500
which had to put together over three times as&nbsp;
many VFX shots as they had for District 9. 
 &nbsp;

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:18.360
When Steven Spielberg decided to adapt&nbsp;
Michael Crichton's novel Jurassic Park,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:22.560
CGI as we know it today didn't&nbsp;
really exist. Spielberg brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.920
on Industrial Light and Magic and&nbsp;
tasked them with creating living,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:30.960
breathing dinosaurs using computer-generated&nbsp;
graphics, and their efforts proved revolutionary.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:36.360
Of course, it wasn't just CGI that brought the&nbsp;
inhabitants of Jurassic Park to life. There were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:41.401
a number of practical effects used too, and ILM&nbsp;
mixed it up in the same way for Jurassic World.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.401 --> 00:06:46.140
The fourth film in the franchise used detailed&nbsp;
white casts of dinosaur heads that would later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:50.940
be layered with CGI for close-up shots, and&nbsp;
they used actors in motion capture suits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:55.920
to make sure their movement seemed real. VFX&nbsp;
supervisor Tim Alexander told Below the Line:

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:00.240
"It gave us a new natural look for the&nbsp;
animation. We ended up casting a person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:05.400
to give us a consistency in the performance. There&nbsp;
were individual people being that raptor. We had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:07.308
suits that they would put on with a tail."

00:07:07.308 --> 00:07:08.907
When George Miller returned to the world of 

00:07:08.907 --> 00:07:14.700
Mad Max with his critically acclaimed Fury&nbsp;
Road in 2015, audiences were blown away by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:18.240
the sequel's frenetic pace and visceral&nbsp;
action—much of which was achieved through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.468
the hard work of inventive mechanics who built&nbsp;
the vehicles used in the movie from scratch.

00:07:22.468 --> 00:07:26.700
The chase scenes were all shot for real,&nbsp;
but the final product wouldn't have looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:31.320
anywhere near as eye-popping if it weren't for&nbsp;
the VFX team. Led by supervisor Andrew Jackson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:35.820
hundreds of CGI artists enhanced&nbsp;
over 2000 shots in Fury Road,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:39.422
from adding characters to&nbsp;
creating an epic toxic storm. 
 &nbsp;

00:07:39.688 --> 00:07:45.180
When Earth's Mightiest Heroes teamed up on the big&nbsp;
screen in 2012, the stakes were high for Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:51.060
Studios and their team of visual effects artists.&nbsp;
Jeff White, the film's VFX supervisor, told MTV:

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:55.740
"With Avengers, there were so many things&nbsp;
to get right. We created a lot of New York&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.740 --> 00:08:01.140
City for the film and needed to build flying&nbsp;
shots of Iron Man all from photography. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:06.120
had to build a new Iron Man suit—the Mark&nbsp;
VII—and Stark Tower. We had to build the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:10.440
alien race. When you add all of those things&nbsp;
up, there are quite a few challenges there."

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:14.880
Green screens were used during most of the&nbsp;
film's action sequences, so the cast spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:19.740
a good chunk of time reacting to invisible&nbsp;
threats and taking cover from fake explosions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:24.660
The biggest hurdle they faced was inserting the&nbsp;
Hulk into group situations. White explained:

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:27.960
"We wanted it to feel very natural&nbsp;
when he's sitting in that circle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:31.920
Avengers. We spent a lot of time working&nbsp;
on his skin and his hair and his teeth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:34.080
just to make sure that all&nbsp;
of that was believable."
 &nbsp;

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:40.140
To create the illusion of flight, Richard Donner's&nbsp;
Superman employed wire rigs to hang leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:44.160
man Christopher Reeve in front of different&nbsp;
projected backgrounds. The film is a classic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:48.300
and the effects looked great in the&nbsp;
'70s, but watching it today it feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:51.900
as though you're flying with Superman as&nbsp;
opposed to him flying past you—something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:56.940
Man of Steel director Zack Snyder wanted to&nbsp;
avoid when he set out to make Superman soar.

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:00.660
Snyder decided to use a handheld approach&nbsp;
to filming Clark's flying scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:05.400
which meant adding in most of the effects&nbsp;
in post. Wire rigs and gimbals were used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:09.420
to suspend Henry Cavill in front of green&nbsp;
screens, and the CGI team did the rest.
 &nbsp;

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:16.320
When the first Iron Man movie dropped in 2008,&nbsp;
director Jon Favreau wasn't known for CGI-heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:20.040
features, but advances in technology&nbsp;
had convinced him to change his stance.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:24.660
"I've always skewed away from using visual&nbsp;
effects whenever possible. That being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:27.480
in the last few years there's been&nbsp;
a lot of wonderful visual effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:31.200
movies where it's beginning to&nbsp;
become seamless even to me."

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:36.780
Favreau returned for the sequel, but Shane Black&nbsp;
took the reins for Iron Man 3, which contained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:41.760
some of the most challenging visual effects yet.&nbsp;
Marvel Studios head honcho Kevin Feige explained:

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.860
"We wanted the ability to be able to suit up&nbsp;
anywhere, anyhow, without a giant gantry."

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:52.740
The answer was having Tony Stark design outlets&nbsp;
he injects just below the skin, not unlike the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:57.480
comics, allowing him to call the Iron Man&nbsp;
suit from anywhere. To pull off the effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:02.820
each individual piece had to be digitally added&nbsp;
to Robert Downey Jr.'s body after the fact.

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:08.520
Years of gymnastics training really paid off for&nbsp;
Tom Holland on the set of Spider-Man: Homecoming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:13.500
the character's eagerly anticipated introduction&nbsp;
to the Marvel Cinematic Universe. Even with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:18.000
advances in CGI in recent years, it helps to&nbsp;
have an actor who feels comfortable jumping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:22.920
around in tights and doesn't mind hanging around&nbsp;
on wires all day long. This was the case with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:27.600
the film's Washington Monument scene—but while&nbsp;
that was indeed Holland under the Spidey mask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:31.680
the monument itself was a fake,&nbsp;
erected on a studio sound stage.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:35.340
"We couldn't film at the real Washington&nbsp;
Monument, but we built very impressive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:38.400
very large chunks of the monument for filming."

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.820
Holland got up the structure&nbsp;
with the aid of a wire rig,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:45.060
though even with his background it&nbsp;
was far from a walk in the park.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:51.600
"We did two weeks and every single shot was&nbsp;
upside down. My head just took a pounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:54.000
from all the blood that was rushing to it."

00:10:54.334 --> 00:10:57.505
The CGI Jon Favreau used for Iron Man was 

00:10:57.505 --> 00:11:02.700
nothing compared to what he did for his 2016&nbsp;
live-action adaptation of The Jungle Book. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:06.840
create an entire jungle around a human&nbsp;
actor, they needed to pull out all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:10.440
stops. The lighting was particularly&nbsp;
difficult. The director explained:

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:15.900
"It's very hard to fake light and shadow.&nbsp;
So everything became about using panels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:19.980
of LEDs to project light so if we had&nbsp;
the kid bowing before the elephants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:23.640
you have panels where we actually&nbsp;
would pre-animate the elephants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:27.300
and they would cast the shadows on&nbsp;
the kid in the exact right way."

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:32.160
This meant that 12-year-old star Neel Sethi had&nbsp;
to imagine the animals he was interacting with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:37.020
though Favreau was on hand to make the experience&nbsp;
as real as possible for the young actor via&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:41.760
puppets and actors in blue suits. 
 
Paul Verhoeven's RoboCop got the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:46.140
out of the special effects available in&nbsp;
'80s, using a combination of stop-motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:49.920
and prosthetic builds to create a movie&nbsp;
that was disturbingly realistic at times.

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:55.020
In the 2014 reboot, Murphy gets his injuries&nbsp;
in an explosion that was created digitally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:57.600
but the suit he dons afterward was actually real.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:02.040
"And it was a philosophy from the start that&nbsp;
we were going to have a head-to-toe suit."

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:07.080
The challenge was building a suit that was mobile&nbsp;
and easy for the digital department to add onto.
 &nbsp;

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:12.360
Ridley Scott is no stranger to special&nbsp;
effects, but creating the red planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:15.660
onscreen for The Martian may have been&nbsp;
his biggest challenge as a director.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:20.220
While some practical effects were used, a&nbsp;
huge amount of digital work was required to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:25.440
give The Martian Scott's desired look. VFX&nbsp;
supervisor Anders Langlands told Gizmodo:

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:29.280
"[Ridley Scott] is famous for doing&nbsp;
his little sketches which are sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:32.580
of really cool Ridley-grams.&nbsp;
We'd ask 'What do you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:36.060
the background mountains to look like in&nbsp;
this shot?' And he'd sketch out a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:40.500
diagram of what they wanted. So you just&nbsp;
literally match that and he'd be happy."

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:45.900
A lot of time was spent finding the right hue for&nbsp;
the skies and arid landscapes of Mars, though in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:50.640
the end it was a simple thing that caused the&nbsp;
VFX team the most problems: the helmet visors.

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:54.864
"But of course glass visors would reflect the crew 
and the lights and the sound stage.

00:12:54.864 --> 00:12:59.696
So all the helmet visors you see in the film are actually added in digitally."

00:12:59.696 --> 00:13:01.627
David Ayer's anti-hero 

00:13:01.627 --> 00:13:07.620
ensemble Suicide Squad definitely had its flaws,&nbsp;
but few of those were the fault of the many visual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:12.600
effects houses—18 companies in total—that&nbsp;
worked tirelessly on it. Imageworks were the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:16.380
ones who handled the Squad's final battle&nbsp;
with Enchantress, and the movie's villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:22.320
proved to be a huge challenge, according to VFX&nbsp;
supervisor Mark Breakspear. He told ARTofVFX:

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:27.300
"Enchantress was a unique challenge as the&nbsp;
actress had been shot without a costume as we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:31.800
had to add it in later to allow it to behave in&nbsp;
a way that normal cloth or materials couldn't."

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:38.580
Breakspear later told AWN that of the 300 shots&nbsp;
they enhanced for the film's third act, dealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:43.620
with the Enchantress' tattoos proved the most&nbsp;
difficult. "That was amazingly tricky to make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:48.780
it looked like skin, but also had the translucency&nbsp;
that we needed to see the sub-surface tattoos."

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:55.080
Watching Martin Scorsese's ode to excess The Wolf&nbsp;
of Wall Street, the only scenes that stick out as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.080 --> 00:14:00.360
being possibly computer-generated are the one&nbsp;
during which Jordan Belfort's yacht sinks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:05.520
the one when a lion wanders freely through his&nbsp;
office. But a visual effects reel released by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:10.440
Brainstorm Digital revealed that some of the most&nbsp;
basic shots in the movie were rendered with CGI.

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:15.780
From sun loungers to tennis courts, Brainstorm&nbsp;
made plenty of subtle touches to bring the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:20.100
in line with the director's vision. VFX&nbsp;
producer Mark Russell told Digital Arts:

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:23.640
"Working with Martin Scorsese,&nbsp;
everything is about propelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:27.240
a story forward and contributing to&nbsp;
the film. I feel that with his movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:31.200
there's a kind of stylized realism to&nbsp;
them that we have to integrate with."

00:14:31.559 --> 00:14:37.020
The amount of destruction on show in this&nbsp;
Dwayne Johnson-led blockbuster definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:42.240
doesn't come cheap, but San Andreas actually&nbsp;
cost a lot less to make than many of the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:46.380
natural disaster flicks that came before it.&nbsp;
According to Variety, the production budget was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:52.740
only $114 million—roughly half the amount needed&nbsp;
to bring Roland Emmerich's 2012 to the big screen.

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:55.680
The film's visual effects&nbsp;
supervisor, Colin Strause,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:00.000
was able to keep costs down by employing&nbsp;
practical solutions to problems that most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:03.900
visual effects companies would solve with&nbsp;
only computers today. As he explained it:

00:15:03.900 --> 00:15:08.340
"You can make a $100 million movie&nbsp;
look like a $200 million movie. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:12.720
can make movies way smarter. CG for&nbsp;
the sake of CG is always a mistake." 
 &nbsp;

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:17.040
Effects studio Weta Digital changed the game&nbsp;
when they helped reboot Planet of the Apes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:21.900
with a new trilogy of prequels, beginning with&nbsp;
2011's Rise of the Planet of the Apes. Producer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:26.100
Dylan Clark had a special amount of praise for&nbsp;
Andy Serkis. The industry's top performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:31.134
capture artist shone as protagonist Caesar,&nbsp;
redefining screen acting in the digital age.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:35.760
"Andy Serkis is unlike any other&nbsp;
actor. He can inhabit characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:39.240
that don't speak and emote in ways&nbsp;
that you don't really see often."

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:42.060
Fox campaigned for Serkis&nbsp;
to get an Oscar nomination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:46.380
but the call from the Academy never came,&nbsp;
much to the actor's frustration. After the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:50.940
release of 2017's War of the Planet&nbsp;
of the Apes, Serkis told Independent:

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:53.940
"The awarding bodies should not&nbsp;
discriminate about this being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:57.300
different. The visual effects render the&nbsp;
character, just like putting on makeup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:01.320
except here it happens after the fact.&nbsp;
It's digital makeup, if you will."

00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:05.820
Bringing J.K. Rowling's Wizarding World to life&nbsp;
was only ever going to be possible with the help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:10.920
of CGI, and the films made increasingly good use&nbsp;
of visual effects with each installment. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:16.500
early CGI hasn't aged well, the quality of digital&nbsp;
imagery from Goblet of Fire onward still holds up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:21.120
The VFX team had to up their game for the fourth&nbsp;
film in the series, as it marked the return of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:25.740
Lord Voldemort. Paul Franklin, the VFX supervisor&nbsp;
for Order of the Phoenix and Half-Blood Prince,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:30.662
revealed that creating Voldemort's nose was&nbsp;
a mammoth task. Franklin told Radio Times:

00:16:30.662 --> 00:16:33.660
"There's this idea that we put the&nbsp;
shots through the computer and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:37.380
just vanished off [his] face. [...] [His&nbsp;
nose] had to be painstakingly edited out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:40.920
frame by frame, over the whole film.&nbsp;
And then the snake slits had to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:44.400
added and tracked very carefully using&nbsp;
dots put on his face for reference."

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:46.740
That's a lot of work for a couple nose slits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.740 --> 00:16:48.720
but Voldemort just wouldn't&nbsp;
have been the same without them.

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:58.114
"Nothing's too good for James Potter."
 &nbsp;

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:03.540
Filming this tale of a boy stranded at sea&nbsp;
with a Bengal tiger came with a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:08.580
obstacles—but the biggest one wasn't the tiger,&nbsp;
it was the water. Director Ang Lee explained:

00:17:08.580 --> 00:17:12.720
"I knew that to make it work with 3D, water&nbsp;
had to become a character itself. I've never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:16.260
seen realistic water scenes in movies&nbsp;
because water hits one side of a tank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:19.800
wall and bounces back like it would in a&nbsp;
bathtub, but we needed to make it work."

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:24.180
That's not to say that the tiger was easy to&nbsp;
create, either. Scenes in which Pi interacts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:28.800
with the deadly animal were done using a crude&nbsp;
puppet. The rest of it had to come from CGI.

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:34.500
Tim Burton was creating stunning visual&nbsp;
effects long before relying heavily on CGI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.500 --> 00:17:39.420
using a combination of practical techniques to&nbsp;
bring his vision to the screen. The former Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:44.700
artist used stop-motion animation, miniatures and&nbsp;
puppetry to great effect in his earlier works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:48.720
though when the opportunity to direct his own&nbsp;
version of Alice In Wonderland came along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:52.740
he soon realized that he couldn't do it&nbsp;
without leaning heavily on digital wizardry.

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:56.100
This meant that all the stars of the&nbsp;
movie had to do the bulk of their acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.100 --> 00:18:00.000
in front of green screens. According to&nbsp;
visual effects supervisor Ken Ralston,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:01.920
it was a challenge for the whole team.

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:07.200
"The green screen color, after long periods of&nbsp;
time, seems to create a certain aggravation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:11.340
irritation after staring at it for a long&nbsp;
period. It is effective for compositing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:15.840
but visually and psychologically exhausting to be&nbsp;
surrounded by it for extended periods of time."

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:20.820
When Disney first announced their intention&nbsp;
to remake a number of their beloved animated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:25.200
classics in live action format, the title&nbsp;
that raised the most eyebrows was Beauty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:29.160
and the Beast. A film in which the majority&nbsp;
of the supporting characters are animated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:34.800
household objects was always going to rely on&nbsp;
CGI, though director Bill Condon has never liked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:39.422
digital effects and actively avoided using&nbsp;
them wherever possible. He told IndieWire:

00:18:39.422 --> 00:18:44.220
"It was important to make everything that could&nbsp;
be real, real. I'm not a big fan of CG movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:49.320
The idea is to feel grounded in a world and not&nbsp;
be distracted by things that felt like additions."

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:51.660
Unfortunately for the actor portraying the Beast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:56.400
Dan Stevens, it was motion capture all the way,&nbsp;
plus some stilts to get him to the right height.

00:18:56.701 --> 00:18:59.935
"It wasn't easy on stilts, heh heh."

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.840
Stevens spoke to both Andy Serkis and&nbsp;
Mark Ruffalo about their experiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:07.320
in motion capture roles, and their&nbsp;
feedback left him sweating. He added:

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:10.620
"I think I was mildly terrified. But&nbsp;
that's really, generally what drives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:13.980
a lot of my decisions these days, is&nbsp;
calibrating the right amount of terror."

00:19:13.980 --> 00:19:17.014
Nothing like a Disney movie to&nbsp;
get that fear meter up, right?

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:21.720
Christopher Nolan is notorious for using&nbsp;
real-life effects as much as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:24.600
and most of the big effects in The&nbsp;
Dark Knight were done on-camera.

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:27.420
"The only visual effect involved in that scene was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:30.600
to remove the ram that they&nbsp;
had built inside the truck."

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:35.940
But The Dark Knight still offered some unique CG&nbsp;
challenges, especially since it was the first film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:41.520
to shoot scenes with IMAX cameras as opposed to&nbsp;
using 35 millimeter and converting in post. Visual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:46.680
effects supervisor Nick Davis spoke to StudioDaily&nbsp;
about his experience on the Batman flick, saying:

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:50.160
"It was important for the visual effects&nbsp;
to look as though they were shot on set.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:53.580
[Nolan] didn't want the film to take&nbsp;
on a gimmicky visual-effects look."

00:19:53.580 --> 00:19:58.440
Taking this route meant that Nolan's first&nbsp;
sequel to 2005's Batman Begins was "a difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:03.401
movie to finish" as the visual effects had to be&nbsp;
rendered in a higher resolution than ever before.

00:20:03.401 --> 00:20:08.040
When it came to creating the look for Harvey&nbsp;
"Two-Face" Dent, Nolan wanted to remove layers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:13.680
not add them. The VFX team had to digitally&nbsp;
remove the skin. Two-Face's exposed eyeball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:18.360
had to be painstakingly animated by hand, as&nbsp;
were the subtle changes in muscle tension.

00:20:18.360 --> 00:20:21.540
The biggest challenge Alfonso&nbsp;
Cuarón faced making his space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.540 --> 00:20:26.220
epic Gravity was achieving a realistic&nbsp;
lack of gravity on screen. Pulling it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:29.580
off involved an intricate mixture&nbsp;
of practical and digital effects.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:36.153
"Now there are flares, and there are mistakes,&nbsp;
as it were, but...no mistakes. It's all created."

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:40.920
The director talked to Space about the challenges&nbsp;
in getting the effects to look realistic, saying:

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:45.660
"It took a lot of education for the animators&nbsp;
to fully grasp that the usual laws of cause and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:49.020
effect don't apply. In outer space,&nbsp;
there is no up, there is no down."

00:20:49.020 --> 00:20:52.080
Co-stars Sandra Bullock and&nbsp;
George Clooney were advised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:55.560
on their movements by real astronauts&nbsp;
before being hooked up to a series of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.560 --> 00:20:59.460
rigs created by Academy Award-winning&nbsp;
visual effects artist Neil Corbould.

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:03.840
It turns out that Bullock not only had to put up&nbsp;
with being suspended from wires most of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.840 --> 00:21:07.680
there were electric shocks to contend&nbsp;
with, too. Corbould explained:

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:10.800
"There was a charge in there like&nbsp;
the static you feel underneath an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:15.360
electricity substation. If you walked out then&nbsp;
touched someone, you'd get this massive shock."

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:17.640
It may not have been easy work for the actors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.640 --> 00:21:21.240
but it worked out pretty well for Corbould,&nbsp;
who won his second Oscar for Gravity.

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:24.480
Arguably one of the most&nbsp;
influential films of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:28.440
The Matrix truly changed the landscape&nbsp;
of action movies, with its memorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:32.700
fight sequences becoming a benchmark for&nbsp;
stunt coordinators and VFX artists alike.

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:37.380
The term "bullet time" was coined for the epic&nbsp;
slow motion shots that the movie employed on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.380 --> 00:21:42.662
regular, which were created by visual effects&nbsp;
guru John Gaeta. Gaeta old The New York Times:

00:21:42.662 --> 00:21:47.580
"The directors wanted to emulate the stylization&nbsp;
found in Japanese animation. When we created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:52.440
weather, fire and smoke, we wanted it to move&nbsp;
not realistically but impressionistically."

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:56.340
Raw footage released as an extra on&nbsp;
the DVD showed how bullet time was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.340 --> 00:22:01.434
achieved using an intricate setup of perfectly&nbsp;
positioned cameras and masses of green screen.

00:22:01.434 --> 00:22:04.500
Whenever Peter Jackson's The Lord&nbsp;
of the Rings trilogy comes up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:08.700
the first thing most people mention is Andy&nbsp;
Serkis' brilliant motion capture performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:12.960
though creating Gollum was just one of many&nbsp;
challenges Weta faced during the movies.

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:17.760
Practical solutions like miniature models&nbsp;
and forced perspective were used in places,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:21.660
but the bulk of the visual effects&nbsp;
were achieved digitally. Even the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.660 --> 00:22:25.440
interior of Bilbo Baggins' hobbit hole&nbsp;
was computer generated for some shots.

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:29.640
Speaking to AWN, visual effects&nbsp;
supervisor Jim Rygiel revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:32.160
that Return of the King was his&nbsp;
favorite of the three movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:37.320
because of the monumental amount of work he and&nbsp;
his team put into the picture. He explained:

00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:40.140
"Return of the King has more&nbsp;
shots than one and two combined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.140 --> 00:22:45.420
it's like 1,450. It was mind-boggling to&nbsp;
figure out logistically how to do them all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:49.380
It's something you never think you could have&nbsp;
done and you did it, like climbing Mt. Everest."

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:54.720
Bill Nighy's character Davy Jones made his&nbsp;
first appearance in 2006's Dead Man's Chest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:58.140
although you may not have recognized the&nbsp;
actor under all those digital tentacles.

00:22:58.140 --> 00:23:04.860
"I was once required to be a squid&nbsp;
man in a movie. I was an octopus guy."

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:08.160
"And I was perfectly happy watching&nbsp;
that. Because what are you going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:12.120
say? You don't like my squid? Well&nbsp;
then, you know...live with it."

00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:16.200
Visual effects supervisor John Knoll was&nbsp;
able to develop a method that allowed Nighy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:20.340
to be on set with his fellow actors instead of&nbsp;
filming separately in front of a green screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.340 --> 00:23:23.580
which in turn allowed him to&nbsp;
interact more believably and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.580 --> 00:23:28.260
deliver a far more genuine performance.&nbsp;
Knoll described the process to EW, saying:

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:32.040
"On a soundstage with 25 technicians&nbsp;
staring at Bill and nobody to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:35.520
off of, that quirkiness would all have&nbsp;
gotten ironed out. Somebody would say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:39.900
that gesture is 'too off' or 'too odd.' It could&nbsp;
have become a real sort of committee effort."

00:23:39.900 --> 00:23:44.040
"My squid was world class.&nbsp;
My squid was an award-winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:47.340
squid because of the hundred men&nbsp;
and women who created my squid."

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:52.140
The post-credits scene in Pirates of the&nbsp;
Caribbean: Dead Men Tell No Tales opened the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:57.300
door for the return of supernatural sealord Davy&nbsp;
Jones, although Nighy had no part in it. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:57.300 --> 00:24:01.980
he had no idea his award-winning squid was even in&nbsp;
the movie until a taxi driver told him about it.

00:24:01.980 --> 00:24:06.360
The season finales on Game of Thrones always&nbsp;
have a cinematic quality, thanks in part to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:11.460
the digital wizardry of Pixomondo. Green screen&nbsp;
use is kept to a minimum where possible, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:11.460 --> 00:24:16.800
bringing the creatures of George R. R. Martin's&nbsp;
world into the mix requires some top-notch CGI.

00:24:16.946 --> 00:24:22.020
Pixomondo's VFX supervisor Sven Martin told&nbsp;
The Washington Post that one of their biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:26.220
achievements has been making Daenerys' dragons&nbsp;
blend seamlessly with the world around them.

00:24:26.220 --> 00:24:31.140
But all that magic doesn't happen until the dragon&nbsp;
scenes have been shot, so how does Emilia Clarke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.140 --> 00:24:35.640
connect with her fire-breathing babies? Green&nbsp;
puppets. The actress told Huffington Post.

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:40.080
"They have these amazing life-scale models&nbsp;
that we used for camera rehearsals for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:44.340
sake of the CGI people, and I got very attached&nbsp;
to them. [...] Fundamentally [they're] dolls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:48.360
I suppose. But it was nice to have something&nbsp;
physical that I could really picture in my mind."

